00:00:33.882 --> 00:00:35.340
MARTIN KRONBERG:
Hello, and welcome

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:38.730
to the third and final
webinar in our series.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.690
My name is Martin Kronberg,
and I am a Technical Evangelist

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:43.375
with Intel.

00:00:43.375 --> 00:00:45.000
KIRAT PANDYA: Hi,
and I'm Kirat Pandya,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.460
Technology Lead for Machine
Learning for Partnerships

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.375
at Google.

00:00:48.375 --> 00:00:50.250
MARTIN KRONBERG: In the
previous two webinars

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:54.360
we looked at how to collect and
send data using our gateway,

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.800
how to query that data when it
gets into GCP using BigQuery,

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.680
and then finally how to
visualize the results in Data

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.060
Studio.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.680
Now, in this
webinar, we're going

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.040
to show you how to use that data
to train a predictive machine

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.710
learning model.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:10.710
KIRAT PANDYA: All right.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.420
So how are we going
to get-- what's

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.050
the story of this webinar?

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.620
So we're going to start with
talking about machine learning

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:17.550
on Google Cloud--

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.250
the high-level tools that
we provide to help you

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.480
build your models.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.970
Next, we're going to talk
about the actual nitty-gritty

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.850
of building a model,
TensorFlow, the math behind it,

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:27.750
all of that.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.990
And then finally, I'm going to
walk you through a code demo

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.000
where we sort of
walk through the code

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.102
that it takes to build
a, model and then

00:01:35.102 --> 00:01:37.560
we're going to show you both
hosting the model in the cloud

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:42.480
as well as on the Edge on
IoT devices and gateways,

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.540
right where things are
happening, if you will.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.020
At this point, you're
probably thinking, well,

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:48.990
I can probably--

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.390
I have the right
equipment under my desk.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:52.950
I can start building
a model here.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:54.120
And you're absolutely right.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.390
However, when you get to
building a model at scale

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.774
to solve real business
problems, your models

00:01:59.774 --> 00:02:00.690
are going to be large.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.760
And what you're going
to need is access

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.586
to thousands of cores of
compute to go train this model.

00:02:06.586 --> 00:02:08.460
You're going to need
tons and tons of memory,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.449
and CPU to go make that happen.

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:12.480
And what Google
Cloud does is we're

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.320
very good at being able to
provision thousands of cores

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.970
in very short amounts of time--

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:23.100
seconds, at most-- to
go build these models.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.240
And we only bill
you by the minute.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.809
So what ends up
happening is instead

00:02:26.809 --> 00:02:28.350
of you having to
worry about how am I

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.630
going to spool up a cluster
and manage all of this,

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.020
we take care of that for you.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:35.280
So instead of planning your
budgets for the next three

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.770
years, how much does my data
science team need in terms

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:42.060
of compute, you can just
sort of use it as necessary

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.190
and get access to
effectively supercomputer

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.680
levels of performance.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.320
Now that we're talking
about building a ML model,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.560
the start of this
journey is data.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.217
You're going to need tons and
tons of data to build models.

00:02:57.217 --> 00:02:59.550
There are some models that
can go off a small data sets.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:04.080
There are some models that
need large data sets to build.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.090
The piece of advice
I would give here

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:10.380
is don't start a journey
of gathering data first.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.390
Start by asking a
business question-- what

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:13.860
is it that I want to solve?

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.770
From there go to
your data sets, go

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.510
to your teams that manage
your data, and make sure

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.850
you have your data that
you already have collected,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.440
cleaned up, where it's
generally understood

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:26.640
there is no sort of random
edge cases in your data.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:27.930
Start with that.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.370
The data is everything.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.340
And companies that know
how to leverage this data

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.080
will get ahead, and companies
that don't will fall behind.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.670
And every large IPO
that we're seeing

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.940
happen in these last few
years has machine learning

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:43.440
and the utilization
of the data they

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.442
have collected as a pillar of
the value prop of that company.

00:03:46.442 --> 00:03:47.400
MARTIN KRONBERG: Right.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.070
Well, like Kirat just
said, data is really

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:52.204
everything when it comes
to machine learning.

00:03:52.204 --> 00:03:54.120
And in fact, when we're
talking about training

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.360
a neural network, we're
going to need a lot of data.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.030
So before moving
on, I kind of want

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:02.830
to talk about how
you, as a developer,

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:06.540
can use Intel IoT hardware
and software to gather

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.940
all the data that we're going
to need for our training.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.520
So in this demo, we're
looking at a use case

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.980
of monitoring three
environmental variables

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.040
in order to detect
anomalies in that data.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.320
We're gathering
temperature, sound,

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:22.770
and light environmental
data using three sensors.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:25.290
Gathering and analyzing this
kind of environmental data

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:28.590
can be extremely useful in many
different commercial sectors--

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.980
so from monitoring a
manufacturing process,

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.620
to a smart building's
HVAC system,

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:38.440
or even keeping track of
individual workers' biometrics.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:41.610
And what we want to do here
is create a prototype system

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:44.340
which can be applicable
to all of these on really

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:45.780
any other application.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:47.850
So in order to gather
the data, we're

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.220
using these Grove
prototype sensors.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.290
And these sensors
are meant for quickly

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:55.050
being able to prototype
an IoT project.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:57.540
They have these
sockets here, which

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.816
get two sensor pins,
a power and a ground.

00:05:00.816 --> 00:05:02.190
And that basically
means that you

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:05.384
can use them without having to
breadboard or solder anything.

00:05:05.384 --> 00:05:07.300
And overall it's going
to make your developing

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.060
process a lot faster.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.880
The sensors are plugged
into an Arduino 101.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:15.370
And the Arduino 101 is
being used as a sensor hub.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:18.630
Basically we're just using it
for its GPIO input as well as

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.655
an analog-to-digital converter.

00:05:20.655 --> 00:05:24.750
All of the control logic
is running here on our NUC.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.540
In order to be able to
read that sensor data in,

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.000
we're using Intel's
MRAA UPM libraries

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.770
All These libraries give
developers open source sensor

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:38.130
APIs that make reading in
sensor data really simple.

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:40.250
We cover all of this
in much greater detail

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:41.527
in our first webinar.

00:05:41.527 --> 00:05:43.110
And if you haven't
already watched it,

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:45.460
I'd recommend going back
and checking it out.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:47.960
What this means in
terms of data gathering

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:50.420
is that you can identify what
the important environmental

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:54.620
data for your application is,
and use inexpensive prototype

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:58.520
sensors as well as a simple
API to read in all that data

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:01.190
quickly and easily,
and then send it up

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:04.610
to the GCP machine learning
service to start building up

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:06.540
your ML model.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.500
KIRAT PANDYA: So now that you've
got the data in the cloud,

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:12.454
and you're ready to go build
a machine learning model,

00:06:12.454 --> 00:06:14.870
the question becomes, what is
this machine learning thing,

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:15.740
right?

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:19.550
Machine learning engineers
like to use really big words

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.110
around concepts
that really, when

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:23.360
you sit down and sort
of understand them, boil

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.070
down to simple ideas.

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.710
So machine learning
is basically where

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:32.300
you take data sets and then
you apply algorithms to extract

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:33.780
an understanding of data.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:36.320
So what used to
be the case of we

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:39.410
had data scientists who
worked on one data set

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:42.920
or a few of them and really
understood the problem space

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:46.910
and spent years being able
to write these long SQL

00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:49.510
queries to go extract that
right business answer.

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:52.550
Instead now we're
saying let's just throw

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:57.230
this algorithm at the problem
and use statistics to extract

00:06:57.230 --> 00:06:59.000
an understanding of this data.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.420
And that's all machine
learning really is.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:06.020
So let's talk about what are
the flavors of machine learning.

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:07.910
Fundamentally, at
Google Cloud we

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:12.560
sort of see two audiences
that use our tooling.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:17.310
On one hand, we have
ready-to-use models.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.340
These are sort of general
problem space models

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.140
where things like
you take a picture,

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:24.290
and you want to identify the
kind of dog in that picture.

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:28.640
And these models are trained
by Google engineers using

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:30.842
Google's massive scale
and massive data sets.

00:07:30.842 --> 00:07:32.300
They're the same
sort of technology

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:35.690
that powers Google translate,
so the same tack if you want it

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:36.450
for your business.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:38.870
That's the ready-to-use models.

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:42.110
Next up we have an
entire tooling pipeline

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:44.480
to help you build
your own models

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.390
and sort of the
target audience there

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:48.530
it's data scientists--
the same folks who

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:50.990
had that deep
understanding of their data

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:55.320
set to go build their own
neural network-based models

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:56.180
end to end.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:59.570
So that includes things like
scalable storage, scalable

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:01.790
databases and warehouses
like BigQuery,

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:04.370
which sort of give you
the same performance

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.260
as your compute rises.

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:09.440
And then we have what's called
our Cloud Machine Learning

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.290
Engine that lets you
provision compute

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:14.840
without sort of doing the
management side of things.

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:17.090
So you can literally
say one line of code,

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:19.760
hey, I want 10,000 cores
to go run this in parallel,

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:21.177
and we'll take care of that.

00:08:21.177 --> 00:08:23.890
MARTIN KRONBERG:
OK, so if you wanted

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:26.900
to recreate some of
these pre-built models

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:30.020
using the tools available,
are they powerful enough

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:32.609
to be able to actually
recreate these models?

00:08:32.609 --> 00:08:33.650
KIRAT PANDYA: Definitely.

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:36.710
So the tooling that we make
available to you, like Cloud

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:42.500
Machine Learning Engine,
is inspired by the tooling

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:44.179
we use internally at Google.

00:08:44.179 --> 00:08:48.680
So just like Kubernetes is
inspired by Google's container

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:50.240
management system called Borg--

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.510
same idea of Cloud Machine
Learning Engine is sort of,

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:56.020
we've gone through
the hard part.

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:58.520
How do you actually do these
things at scale and production?

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:01.010
And then we've brought the
right pieces, the learnings,

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:03.290
to the table for
you to just use.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:04.990
So the short
answer-- yes, you can

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:07.490
go rebuild the same thing as
long as you have the right data

00:09:07.490 --> 00:09:08.210
sets.

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.293
And that's sort of where
the differentiation comes

00:09:10.293 --> 00:09:15.010
in is that, for general
cases that are like dog, cat,

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:19.640
the example you always see--
hot dog, not hot dog, right?

00:09:19.640 --> 00:09:21.770
Identifying the usual-- exactly.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:25.280
Those general cases,
our APIs do really well.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:27.430
When you're talking about
things that are like,

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:31.940
I took a photo of a part of my
production line in my business,

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:34.730
and I want to have a model
that understands that, I think

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:37.270
that's where you're
starting to look into,

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:38.450
OK, I need to build a model.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:40.290
I need my own data
set to go do that.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:43.330
And that's where the
toolchain part comes in.

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:46.250
So going back to the
machine learning APIs,

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.470
we call these
pre-trained models APIs

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:51.470
because you interact with
them as easily as you

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:52.820
would any web API.

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.590
These APIs make
available to the public

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:59.860
the machine learning models that
were built by Google experts.

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:02.480
They're trained, as I said
earlier, on our massive data

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.420
sets, and heavy-duty
machine learning

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:08.570
with thousands of cores is
going on behind the scenes.

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:12.770
But you don't need to learn any
machine learning to use these.

00:10:12.770 --> 00:10:15.630
We currently have
five, more coming soon.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:18.720
So let's do a quick journey
of the five of them.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:20.750
First up, the Vision API.

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:23.930
This tells you
information about images.

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:26.960
You give it an image, and
it will detect and label it,

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.100
saying, hey, here's a dog.

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:31.000
Here's an Alaskan
malamute in this picture.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.209
It detects faces and
does sentiment analysis.

00:10:33.209 --> 00:10:34.750
So if you're thinking
along the lines

00:10:34.750 --> 00:10:39.580
of, hey, when photos of my
brand show up in a Twitter feed

00:10:39.580 --> 00:10:41.920
or along a hashtag, are
people happy or sad?

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:44.590
Do people generally correlate
happiness with my brand?

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:46.540
You can start extracting this.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:48.370
And then finally,
it extracts text

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:51.710
from images and flags
inappropriate content.

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:54.430
So if you're doing
OCR at scale you

00:10:54.430 --> 00:10:56.500
can use our API to
extract the text

00:10:56.500 --> 00:10:59.250
and then get relevant
business data back out of it.

00:10:59.250 --> 00:11:00.958
MARTIN KRONBERG: And
so, once again, this

00:11:00.958 --> 00:11:03.820
was trained using
the Google image

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:05.597
repository, which is massive.

00:11:05.597 --> 00:11:06.430
KIRAT PANDYA: Right.

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:09.280
And there is sort of this
feedback loop going on there

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:13.090
where the models are helping
you find the images which

00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:16.506
we're then using to make our
original model even better.

00:11:16.506 --> 00:11:18.130
So it's a feedback
loop that just keeps

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:19.213
getting better and better.

00:11:19.213 --> 00:11:21.130
And you just have to
use an API, and you

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:24.730
get the power of Google
for your business case.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:25.300
All right.

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:27.400
Next up, Natural Language API.

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:30.970
The Natural Language API is
all about understanding text.

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:33.430
It will extract and
label important keywords.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:36.760
It does sentiment analysis
just like the face analysis.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:40.270
It tells you if the email
you got to your support queue

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:43.480
has a really angry customer
mad about your product,

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:46.120
and you can prioritize it
instead of auto-responding

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:49.000
to the customer saying,
hey, we will get back

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:52.557
to you in 24 hours that
your auto response is.

00:11:52.557 --> 00:11:54.640
Maybe you can send them
back a coupon code saying,

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:57.640
hey, we recognize that you're
angry, we're prioritizing,

00:11:57.640 --> 00:11:58.360
we're sorry.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:11:59.470
Here you go.

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:03.400
And that potentially saves a
lifelong customer relationship.

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:06.250
Put it together, the API
is useful for analyzing

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:09.400
what people are saying about
your products at scale.

00:12:09.400 --> 00:12:12.700
It helps you support
multiple languages

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:14.620
with advanced syntax analysis.

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:18.010
So case in point, you get an
email to your English support

00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:22.930
queue, but it's someone
asking a question in Spanish.

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:24.670
Instead of just
saying, sorry, we only

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:26.740
support English as a
response, we can now

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:30.320
come back and have at least
a semi-helpful response

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:34.570
in addition to saying, maybe,
we don't do Spanish support

00:12:34.570 --> 00:12:35.740
directly.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:37.630
Next up, the Speech API.

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:39.490
This Speech API is
for voice recognition,

00:12:39.490 --> 00:12:41.620
it is the same core
technology that powers

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:44.850
Google Voice search, Google
Home, Google Assistant.

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:47.200
We've put in billions
of dollars of effort

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.440
into building this into
internationalization,

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.660
and we now support 80 languages.

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:55.690
It is robust to noise,
has a fast streaming

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:59.210
API so you can literally build
similar scenarios as the Google

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:00.010
Assistant.

00:13:00.010 --> 00:13:02.570
And you can customize it
by passing it word hints.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:04.060
So if you have
specific words that

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:06.910
are common in your industry,
you can have a tweaked version

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:09.700
of the API that will understand
that and give you back

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:10.970
the text result.

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:13.050
Next up, the Translation API.

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:16.090
The Translation API
translates over 100 languages

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:18.400
and thousands of language
pairs back and forth

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:19.630
between each other.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:22.900
We launched a premium edition
of this a little while ago,

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:24.520
and one of the
breakthroughs Google

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:27.790
has had in this space is
earlier in the year we started

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:30.190
launching our deep neural
network-based models

00:13:30.190 --> 00:13:31.520
for translation.

00:13:31.520 --> 00:13:34.120
So before that
happened, everybody

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:37.150
who was doing translation was
primarily doing translation

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:40.180
using sort of phrase-based
classical machine learning

00:13:40.180 --> 00:13:41.110
models.

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:44.290
Google literally spends
weeks and months training

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:45.610
neural network models now.

00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:50.830
Real-time translation,
we're getting to the point

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:55.810
where we can support the same
accuracy as a human would

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:57.620
be translating real-time.

00:13:57.620 --> 00:14:00.230
So there's some amazing
stuff going on there.

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:02.510
Next up is the Beta Video API.

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:06.130
So at Google Next
2017 in San Francisco,

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:10.150
we announced an ML API for
extracting insights from video.

00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:11.740
The first set of
this functionality

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:16.180
lets you analyze a video
across the time dimension

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:19.540
and say, from a given
time stamp to another,

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:20.900
what is happening in a video.

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:22.441
So you can say, hey,
there is a tiger

00:14:22.441 --> 00:14:26.080
in this video from
time stamp x to x

00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:27.950
plus y, whatever that may be.

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:30.820
And so that's an API that we
announced recently, and were

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:32.140
continuing to iterate on.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:34.330
So expect more
information on that soon.

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:37.360
Putting all of that together,
the best part of all of this

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:38.939
is you don't need an account.

00:14:38.939 --> 00:14:40.480
You don't need to
go set up anything.

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:42.610
You can try these
all in the browser.

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:44.410
Literally you just
click, click, go.

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:46.670
So I encourage you to play
with them on your own.

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:49.960
It takes 10 minutes to validate
the effectiveness of everything

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:50.800
that I just said.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:52.930
So keep me honest.

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:54.655
The barrier to entry
is extremely low.

00:14:54.655 --> 00:14:56.530
MARTIN KRONBERG: Right,
and once you guys are

00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:59.140
done exploring these
in the browser,

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:01.690
you might want to learn
about how to implement them

00:15:01.690 --> 00:15:03.460
on the gateway.

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:05.080
And as Kirat said,
there's really

00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:08.590
no reason to create your own
custom model if a model already

00:15:08.590 --> 00:15:11.410
exists, and it does
what you need to do.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:13.060
So as an example,
let's take a look

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:16.180
at how we can use that
speech-to-text model

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:19.870
to transcribe a piece of
recorded audio on our NUC

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:21.497
gateway.

00:15:21.497 --> 00:15:23.080
So for this example,
we're going to be

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:27.520
using the GCP SDK for Python
to interface with the speech

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:29.053
recognition model.

00:15:29.053 --> 00:15:32.542
Since the gateway does not
come with GCP SDK by default,

00:15:32.542 --> 00:15:34.500
we're going to have to
install and configure it

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:36.240
before getting started.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:39.690
So we're going to install
the Cloud SDK and then

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:41.040
the Python module.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:45.390
We can do that by simply using
apt-get for the Cloud SDK

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:49.270
and then pip to install
the Python module.

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:51.540
Now, it's important that
you initialize the SDK

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:54.500
before trying to
communicate with GCP.

00:15:54.500 --> 00:15:57.600
In Webinar 1, we
authorized our connection

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:03.210
to the IoT core service by
using a public/private key pair

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:05.000
that we had to generate.

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:08.100
Here we're going to be using
the SDK to authorize access

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:11.220
to the Google machine
learning models,

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.100
so no need for
generating your own keys.

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:17.340
You can do that from the
command line interface.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:20.850
Basically run G Cloud in
it, navigate to that URL

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:23.550
that's generated, and log in.

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:27.180
Once you log in and allow the
SDK to access Google services,

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:30.660
you can set a default
geographic zone if you want.

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:33.480
And that's all you need to do
in order to set up and authorize

00:16:33.480 --> 00:16:34.660
the SDK.

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:36.810
Once the SDK is set
up, we can write

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.030
our script that will
send an audio file up

00:16:39.030 --> 00:16:41.520
to GCP for processing.

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:44.470
The API itself is pretty simple.

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:46.170
We basically import
the speech package

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:50.880
from google.cloud module,
create a speech client,

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:55.050
read in our audio file, set the
parameters of that audio file,

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:58.321
and then send the audio
file to GCP for a response.

00:16:58.321 --> 00:16:59.820
Now, keep in mind
that there's going

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:02.880
to be a few extra bits of
code to make this all work.

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:05.339
But in principle, this
is all that you need.

00:17:05.339 --> 00:17:07.920
Also, in this example
were sending a local file

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:09.700
to the speech recognition model.

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:12.569
But it is also possible to
send a remote file stored

00:17:12.569 --> 00:17:17.520
on Google Cloud or a streaming
file using a microphone--

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.410
pretty powerful stuff
that's wrapped up

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:21.150
in a really simple API.

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.579
KIRAT PANDYA: So lets get
into the details of the latest

00:17:23.579 --> 00:17:24.300
wave in ML--

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:26.430
the resurgence of
neural networks.

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:28.560
Neural networks are
a construct built

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:31.940
by connecting lots of
perceptrons together,

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:33.930
or neurons, if you will.

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:38.025
A perceptron is just a function
that has a conditional value.

00:17:38.025 --> 00:17:42.480
A perceptron can be
designed to effectively host

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.600
any mathematical function
to calculate a value.

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:47.220
That's all it is really.

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:50.190
So chaining a bunch
of these together

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:54.900
lets us build complex
high-dimensional relationships

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:57.690
in the model to
capture from the data.

00:17:57.690 --> 00:18:00.120
A neural network
ML model is just

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:03.690
a structure of perceptrons
with each layer performing

00:18:03.690 --> 00:18:05.700
a specific operation
and then feeding

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:07.680
its output to the next layer.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:10.710
In the case of TensorFlow,
the actual asset

00:18:10.710 --> 00:18:13.110
is a model file
that, once trained,

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:15.090
is portable and reusable.

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:17.310
The data scientist's
job is to figure out

00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:20.160
the best structure
of this ML model

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:25.050
that results in a
model that can capture

00:18:25.050 --> 00:18:28.230
the patterns, the statistical
patterns within the data

00:18:28.230 --> 00:18:30.000
so that you can then
use that to predict

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:32.040
future statistical patterns.

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:33.000
That's neural networks.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:34.410
What is deep learning?

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:37.170
Neural networks,
as an ML technique,

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:39.390
actually fell out of
favor a couple of days

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:41.850
after the original
Minsky paper came out.

00:18:41.850 --> 00:18:44.149
And part of the
reason for that was we

00:18:44.149 --> 00:18:45.690
did not have enough
computer hardware

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:49.080
to actually solve any real
significant problems using

00:18:49.080 --> 00:18:50.310
that pattern.

00:18:50.310 --> 00:18:52.980
This has changed rapidly
in the last decade or so,

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:54.900
leading to the rise
of deep learning.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.990
A deep learning model is nothing
more than a neural network

00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:00.030
with lots and lots of layers.

00:19:00.030 --> 00:19:03.000
Were talking in some cases,
with some of the larger Google

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:06.030
models now, millions
and millions of neurons

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:08.070
making hundreds of layers.

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:10.795
There are multiple ML
techniques to train

00:19:10.795 --> 00:19:12.600
these deep neural networks.

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:15.300
So lets step in to each
one of them in terms of,

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:17.880
how would you train one
of these large networks?

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:20.220
So first up is
supervised learning

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:24.600
or where you learn by
example, when you think about,

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.490
hey, I have a big
data set where I

00:19:26.490 --> 00:19:30.240
know the right answer for
each set of input problem data

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:31.100
points.

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:33.380
That's where supervised
learning comes in.

00:19:33.380 --> 00:19:35.580
Second, there is
unsupervised learning.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:38.580
Here you rely on the ML
model to find and predict

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:41.880
patterns in data where
there isn't necessarily

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:43.080
a right answer.

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:46.560
An example would be, by
looking at people moving

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:49.530
around in a store, can
you predict shoplifting?

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:52.140
and to do that,
you're effectively

00:19:52.140 --> 00:19:54.300
trying to build clustering
in terms of movement

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:56.760
patterns of how people
move in the store

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:59.700
and see relationships
form out of that.

00:19:59.700 --> 00:20:03.430
Third-- sort of the most
exciting ongoing research--

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:05.280
we have reinforcement learning.

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:08.500
This technique utilizes top
trial and error patterns

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:11.640
where the computer learns
by actually doing the task.

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:13.680
Again, while the core
research in this space

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:17.760
goes back decades, we're seeing
a resurgence of its application

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:20.940
in new groundbreaking
research emerging now

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:23.090
because of the
availability of compute,

00:20:23.090 --> 00:20:25.830
of the cloud in some ways,
because that compute doesn't

00:20:25.830 --> 00:20:27.240
have to be
pre-purchased upfront.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:29.170
You, again, just get
as much as you need

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:30.370
for the period you need it.

00:20:30.370 --> 00:20:31.328
MARTIN KRONBERG: Right.

00:20:31.328 --> 00:20:34.700
So for instance, when Google
image search returns something

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:36.620
that you're not
expecting, that's

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:40.769
actually a way of it learning
what it should be re--

00:20:40.769 --> 00:20:41.810
KIRAT PANDYA: Yes and no.

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:43.552
So that's actually a good point.

00:20:43.552 --> 00:20:45.260
I should highlight
the difference, right?

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:47.300
When I'm talking about
learning real-time,

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:52.090
what I'm talking about is the
case of, think of it as a robot

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:56.120
that I can teach to manipulate
objects where the robot isn't

00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:59.020
explicitly given a
set of instructions

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:02.000
or, here's how you move
an arm closer to the ball.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:07.010
The robot just is looking at a
ball in its arm, and it learns.

00:21:07.010 --> 00:21:08.120
It's crazy as it sounds.

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:10.490
It learns to figure out
how to pick up the ball

00:21:10.490 --> 00:21:12.380
and put it in the hole.

00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:13.910
That is what we're
talking about.

00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:15.950
What you're talking
about is sort

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:17.900
of a concept, that
overloaded term,

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:22.190
called online learning,
where real-time,

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:24.560
as humans give feedback
to the pattern,

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:27.150
the model itself
improves and knows that,

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:29.880
hey this picture that I thought
was a dog is not really a dog.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:31.880
But that's not directly
reinforcement learning.

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:32.750
MARTIN KRONBERG: Mm.

00:21:32.750 --> 00:21:34.340
KIRAT PANDYA: So
another good example

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:36.710
of reinforcement learning
was was successfully used

00:21:36.710 --> 00:21:44.870
by the Google DeepMind
team to be the top Go

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:47.870
player in the world.

00:21:47.870 --> 00:21:51.230
The computer was able to figure
out unconventional moves.

00:21:51.230 --> 00:21:53.510
People were watching
the live stream.

00:21:53.510 --> 00:21:55.730
At that particular
move, they all

00:21:55.730 --> 00:21:57.710
go, wait, that move
makes no sense.

00:21:57.710 --> 00:22:00.880
Nobody should be doing that
move in that particular case.

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:02.510
And the computer
did that because it

00:22:02.510 --> 00:22:05.390
could predict the outcome
of the entire game all

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:06.290
the way to the end.

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:08.570
And that was taught by
reinforcement learning.

00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:11.810
And that now has gotten
better and better

00:22:11.810 --> 00:22:14.330
with the computer playing
against itself to get better,

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:16.100
because there
weren't humans that

00:22:16.100 --> 00:22:18.620
were good enough to beat it.

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:21.230
So that's sort of where we are
with reinforcement learning.

00:22:21.230 --> 00:22:23.900
All right, so let's talk
about some common machine

00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:24.900
learning tasks.

00:22:24.900 --> 00:22:26.300
There's a list up there.

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:27.230
Let's go through that.

00:22:27.230 --> 00:22:30.640
Classification--
so dog or a cat,

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:31.820
going back to that example.

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:34.790
What kind of bolt is this,
if it's an industry-specific

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:35.630
model--

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:38.690
classes of objects and trying
to figure out what fits where.

00:22:38.690 --> 00:22:40.439
Next up, regression--

00:22:40.439 --> 00:22:42.230
Think of it as you have
a graph, and you're

00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:45.750
trying to predict the direction
of that line in the graph,

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:46.790
the next few steps.

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:49.700
That's sort of what
you're talking about.

00:22:49.700 --> 00:22:52.640
Then there's similarity,
where I have a picture

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:54.680
of an Alaskan malamute.

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:57.080
Show me pictures of more
Alaskan malamutes and not

00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:58.340
Siberian huskies.

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:01.320
That's sort of similarity there.

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:03.350
Ranking-- you see a
lot of this, and you

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:05.390
go to go purchase
something online

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:09.080
and how does the back
end system decide

00:23:09.080 --> 00:23:12.740
that it should show you
what list of products,

00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:15.020
but not just that,
but in what order?

00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:16.650
Which one should be at the top?

00:23:16.650 --> 00:23:18.233
Because that's the
highest probability

00:23:18.233 --> 00:23:20.580
you're going to probably see
it, click it, purchase it--

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:22.280
so ranking.

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:25.100
And then there's sequence
prediction, where--

00:23:25.100 --> 00:23:25.760
type ahead.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:30.260
So can I start pre-typing
for you, like what you see--

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.890
your phone suggests the next few
words and sequence prediction.

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:36.380
Or again, the demo we're
going to show, where can you

00:23:36.380 --> 00:23:38.390
predict the temperature
readout of a sensor

00:23:38.390 --> 00:23:40.280
to then detect anomalies?

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:43.490
And then the last but,
again, not the least,

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:46.670
dimensionality reduction, where,
say, you have lots of options,

00:23:46.670 --> 00:23:48.294
you want to pick
one or pick few.

00:23:48.294 --> 00:23:50.210
So this sort of goes
hand-in-hand in some ways

00:23:50.210 --> 00:23:54.230
with the prediction, the
retail example that I gave.

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:57.320
All right so one of the core
tenets of machine learning

00:23:57.320 --> 00:23:59.960
is anytime you're
trying to build a model,

00:23:59.960 --> 00:24:02.040
you'll end up dealing
with cost functions.

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:04.370
So this is sort of where we
get into the nitty-gritty

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:06.470
of the math of
this, if you will.

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:09.500
A cost function basically
quantifies the delta

00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:11.990
between what a model
predicted versus

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:15.500
the truth, or the reality,
that it's trying to model.

00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:18.830
The process of training a model
is basically an iterative thing

00:24:18.830 --> 00:24:24.110
where you take lots of
predictions from the model,

00:24:24.110 --> 00:24:27.470
you calculate this loss,
this cost, if you will.

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:29.570
I'm overloading terms, but
effectively think of it

00:24:29.570 --> 00:24:31.580
as I calculate that delta.

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:33.890
And then you go back
and adjust that model

00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:38.420
so that the inner weights,
if you will, of the model

00:24:38.420 --> 00:24:41.060
are adjusted to
reduce that loss.

00:24:41.060 --> 00:24:43.437
And then you start
again, do it again

00:24:43.437 --> 00:24:45.770
with the next batch of data,
and the next batch of data.

00:24:45.770 --> 00:24:47.900
And as you can imagine,
hundreds of thousands

00:24:47.900 --> 00:24:49.460
of these iterations
later, the model

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:51.440
gets very good at,
given a set of inputs,

00:24:51.440 --> 00:24:53.060
it gives you that
set of outputs.

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:55.564
So how do you reduce this loss?

00:24:55.564 --> 00:24:57.230
One of the ways to
reduce loss functions

00:24:57.230 --> 00:24:59.680
is to use an algorithm
called gradient descent.

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:03.110
I'll skip the mathematical
details of the algorithm

00:25:03.110 --> 00:25:05.270
here but for one point.

00:25:05.270 --> 00:25:08.870
The algorithm itself is
relatively simple to implement,

00:25:08.870 --> 00:25:12.740
but implementing it
across multiple machines

00:25:12.740 --> 00:25:16.280
to build a modern large-scale
model is very hard.

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:18.789
You have to solve all sorts
of problems and challenges

00:25:18.789 --> 00:25:21.205
around managing the cluster,
connecting them together, all

00:25:21.205 --> 00:25:22.570
of that craziness.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:24.590
TensorFlow makes this
easier while also

00:25:24.590 --> 00:25:27.470
providing other
scenario-specific reduction

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:28.617
algorithms.

00:25:28.617 --> 00:25:30.450
Again, I'm not going
to go into detail here,

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:33.570
but you have a list
of choices where

00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:35.170
one might be better
than another,

00:25:35.170 --> 00:25:37.440
depending on the exact
model and that's where

00:25:37.440 --> 00:25:39.600
the data scientist comes in.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:42.567
You can use these pre-built
models, and algorithms,

00:25:42.567 --> 00:25:44.400
and libraries that are
in TensorFlow to make

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:46.740
your engineering
process easier so

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:49.100
that your devs, and
your data scientists,

00:25:49.100 --> 00:25:50.850
and your business
people can work together

00:25:50.850 --> 00:25:54.020
to solve the business
problem in data science,

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:57.540
and not necessarily the, how
do I bring a set of machines

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:00.000
together to work together
to solve an algorithm?

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:03.540
Now, let's say your along your
journey of building this model.

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:06.990
One other thing is to be very
careful about is overfitting.

00:26:06.990 --> 00:26:09.210
No model is 100% accurate.

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:11.400
Building a good model
involves trading off

00:26:11.400 --> 00:26:15.090
between multiple metrics to
prevent, again, overfitting.

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:18.090
Overfitting is where the
model, after training,

00:26:18.090 --> 00:26:21.040
is over-specialized
for the training data

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:24.870
but not generalized enough
to be useful for new data.

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:26.790
One of the techniques
used for example

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:29.820
here is regularization,
which imposes additional cost

00:26:29.820 --> 00:26:31.790
on model complexity.

00:26:31.790 --> 00:26:35.610
My higher point here
is that in this space

00:26:35.610 --> 00:26:39.720
as well, there are some high
level things that your data

00:26:39.720 --> 00:26:42.240
scientist has to be
aware of, but there

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:44.740
are some programmatic things
that you can do as well.

00:26:44.740 --> 00:26:47.280
In TensorFlow has
tooling for you

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:50.517
to go do that in
a way that says,

00:26:50.517 --> 00:26:52.350
hey, call a couple of
functions, and then it

00:26:52.350 --> 00:26:54.550
takes care of that
problem for you.

00:26:54.550 --> 00:26:59.370
So this goes back to the larger
set of choosing the right tools

00:26:59.370 --> 00:27:01.290
and not building them all.

00:27:01.290 --> 00:27:04.560
So as you can sense,
there is a theme here.

00:27:04.560 --> 00:27:06.720
Let's talk about the
theme, the tools, right?

00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:09.840
You don't build your own
library for measuring time.

00:27:09.840 --> 00:27:12.307
The Y2K bug-- go
back two decades--

00:27:12.307 --> 00:27:13.890
the reason that
happened was everybody

00:27:13.890 --> 00:27:16.440
was writing their own
date calculation function.

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:18.741
And it's complex, and it's hard.

00:27:18.741 --> 00:27:19.490
And you know what?

00:27:19.490 --> 00:27:21.630
There's no business
value in doing that.

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:24.210
So instead of going
to do that, rely

00:27:24.210 --> 00:27:27.290
on something that does the
mundane for you so you can

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:29.380
solve the high-value problem.

00:27:29.380 --> 00:27:32.910
So why TensorFlow then?

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:37.000
There are other options that
have lots of libraries as well.

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:38.770
Well, here's the thing.

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:41.390
Of the other tooling
chains that are available,

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:43.920
TensorFlow is the most popular.

00:27:43.920 --> 00:27:45.840
This right here is a
measure by GitHub stars.

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:48.570
We have massive amounts
of public contribution,

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:50.490
we work directly
with the community,

00:27:50.490 --> 00:27:53.310
and it's the fastest-growing
sort of community platform

00:27:53.310 --> 00:27:54.739
for machine learning today.

00:27:54.739 --> 00:27:56.280
MARTIN KRONBERG: So
is a model that's

00:27:56.280 --> 00:28:00.330
trained in TensorFlow compatible
with the other tooling

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:01.280
frameworks?

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:03.720
KIRAT PANDYA:
Actually, yes, it is.

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:05.700
So let me give you an example.

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:08.340
The Keras API is
a higher level API

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:11.820
that lets you sort of simplify
even some of the dialects

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:15.000
that TensorFlow uses to build
certain kinds of models.

00:28:15.000 --> 00:28:17.760
That API is something,
for example, Apple now

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:22.520
supports in their
core AI platform--

00:28:22.520 --> 00:28:23.190
core ML.

00:28:23.190 --> 00:28:23.910
I'm sorry

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:27.150
So you can train using Keras.

00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:29.190
The end result is
a TensorFlow model,

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:32.220
but this can now run on an
iPhone in Apple's stack.

00:28:32.220 --> 00:28:35.880
So one of the core tenants
of the TensorFlow design

00:28:35.880 --> 00:28:38.820
was you could swap out
the underlying execution

00:28:38.820 --> 00:28:41.010
environment, but
the model itself

00:28:41.010 --> 00:28:43.650
is a self-contained asset
that can run anywhere.

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:46.440
So previously it used to be
you would deploy something

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:48.240
like Spark and then
have an algorithm you

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:49.364
wrote that trained a model.

00:28:49.364 --> 00:28:51.540
But then portability was
sort of an afterthought.

00:28:51.540 --> 00:28:54.060
And this was put right up front.

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:56.310
Actually, that sort of brings
me back to the point of,

00:28:56.310 --> 00:28:57.630
why is it so popular?

00:28:57.630 --> 00:29:00.480
Because it's flexible
enough for research,

00:29:00.480 --> 00:29:03.570
but performant and portable
enough for production.

00:29:03.570 --> 00:29:06.090
It's very rare to have
a single framework that

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:08.220
does both of these things well.

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:11.100
Traditionally you'd
model your problem

00:29:11.100 --> 00:29:13.140
on a developer-friendly
framework,

00:29:13.140 --> 00:29:15.926
and then you would come back,
and your production team

00:29:15.926 --> 00:29:17.550
would say, OK, we
get the math of this.

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:20.690
Let us write this in a
way that actually scales.

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:23.220
TensorFlow takes care of
that because the model

00:29:23.220 --> 00:29:28.860
lets you go from an environment
where you're ID iterating,

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:30.940
coming up with maybe
this model works better,

00:29:30.940 --> 00:29:34.890
and then just taking that asset
and running it in production.

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:36.690
The popularity of
TensorFlow also

00:29:36.690 --> 00:29:39.870
means that you'll have a vibrant
community of fellow developers

00:29:39.870 --> 00:29:43.410
to reach out and get for
help, which, for example,

00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:46.080
Stack Overflow, we've got--

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:48.420
our engineers are
reaching out and working

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:49.590
with this community.

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:52.620
So if you do get stuck on
something, we're there,

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:54.610
or the community
is there to help.

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:55.110
All right.

00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:58.230
So let's get into the specifics
of the model we built here.

00:29:58.230 --> 00:30:00.240
We built what's called an LSTM.

00:30:00.240 --> 00:30:01.650
It's a long short-term memory.

00:30:01.650 --> 00:30:03.540
It's a deep neural
network pattern

00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:07.860
where the model can effectively
build the concept of a memory.

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:09.960
It's not a stateful
machine, per se,

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:15.150
but it has the concept of
taking a set of data points

00:30:15.150 --> 00:30:18.300
from the past and then being
able to predict the next data

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:22.440
point, because it can look at
a sequence in the input data.

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:26.320
This pattern is often used
in situations where there is

00:30:26.320 --> 00:30:28.340
some periodicity to the data.

00:30:28.340 --> 00:30:31.080
There's a rhythm, maybe there's
a sequence, any of that.

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:33.030
And that's what
makes it a great fit

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:35.910
for detecting anomalies and
streaming IoT sensor data,

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:38.100
because there is a periodicity.

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:39.810
Your temperature
goes up, goes down.

00:30:39.810 --> 00:30:41.760
There is a rhythm of
business that applies,

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:43.470
and an LSTM can learn that.

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:44.550
So how does that work?

00:30:44.550 --> 00:30:46.770
The way this works
is that the model

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.620
is trained by giving it a set
amount of time slices of data

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:53.280
and getting it to predict
the next few steps.

00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:54.810
So when you're
doing that, you're

00:30:54.810 --> 00:30:57.930
doing that over lots and
lots of time slices of data.

00:30:57.930 --> 00:31:01.050
Then at runtime similar
inputs are provided real-time,

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:04.182
and the model predicts
the next few data points.

00:31:04.182 --> 00:31:05.640
These next few data
points are then

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:08.220
compared to the real-world
measurement using

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:10.590
multiple methods like
thresholds, accumulators,

00:31:10.590 --> 00:31:14.370
and Gaussian techniques
relying on variants.

00:31:14.370 --> 00:31:17.389
Once the delta between
the model prediction, what

00:31:17.389 --> 00:31:18.930
the model thinks
should be happening,

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:22.110
and the real world triggers
whatever your threshold

00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:24.840
algorithm is, your
measure is, now

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:27.270
you know you have an
anomaly, because over the set

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:29.680
of the clean data you trained
it on, that didn't happen.

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:31.710
So let's say you had
a temperature sensor,

00:31:31.710 --> 00:31:34.650
and it expects a gentle
shift in temperature,

00:31:34.650 --> 00:31:35.574
and there is a fire.

00:31:35.574 --> 00:31:37.740
Well, the temperature is
going to shift really fast.

00:31:37.740 --> 00:31:39.439
It's going to trigger
an anomaly, right?

00:31:39.439 --> 00:31:41.730
MARTIN KRONBERG: So basically
the difference between it

00:31:41.730 --> 00:31:46.034
getting warmer throughout
the day versus it spiking up?

00:31:46.034 --> 00:31:46.950
KIRAT PANDYA: Exactly.

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:47.610
Exactly.

00:31:47.610 --> 00:31:49.320
And then the more
sophisticated you

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:51.141
get with the actual
detection side

00:31:51.141 --> 00:31:52.890
of things-- so there
a prediction and then

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:54.485
a detection.

00:31:54.485 --> 00:31:55.860
And there are
techniques like you

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:58.080
can use a neural network
itself for the detection

00:31:58.080 --> 00:31:59.100
side of things.

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:01.100
But putting all
of that together,

00:32:01.100 --> 00:32:03.600
what it helps you do is instead
of the old system of setting

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:06.720
a threshold of if it gets
above 90 degrees Celsius,

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:07.830
there's probably a fire.

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:11.490
Instead of that, let's say
your server room does get hot.

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:13.950
It has big swings in
temperature for whatever reason,

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:15.900
just hypothetically.

00:32:15.900 --> 00:32:17.170
Your model understands that.

00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:18.800
So it doesn't just
trigger every time

00:32:18.800 --> 00:32:22.050
there's an event
that is expected

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:24.230
to happen on the third
Tuesday of every month

00:32:24.230 --> 00:32:25.630
or something like that.

00:32:25.630 --> 00:32:27.392
You can train that
into the model.

00:32:27.392 --> 00:32:28.350
MARTIN KRONBERG: Right.

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:29.350
KIRAT PANDYA: All right.

00:32:29.350 --> 00:32:31.470
So let's jump right
into the code here.

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:33.990
What I have in front of me
is Google Cloud Datalab,

00:32:33.990 --> 00:32:40.680
which is an open-source version
of the Jupiter notebooks, which

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:43.260
have been modified to just make
it easy for you to do things

00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:45.365
like push jobs
into Google Cloud,

00:32:45.365 --> 00:32:47.490
like solve the authentication
challenges and things

00:32:47.490 --> 00:32:48.227
like that.

00:32:48.227 --> 00:32:49.450
All right.

00:32:49.450 --> 00:32:52.650
So what's on screen right now
is my folder of code files

00:32:52.650 --> 00:32:54.660
that is used to
train this model.

00:32:54.660 --> 00:32:56.850
There is the model
file, model.py,

00:32:56.850 --> 00:33:00.540
which defines the TensorFlow
graph for the model itself.

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:02.260
The setup file, as
the name indicates,

00:33:02.260 --> 00:33:04.620
is setting up
dependencies, things

00:33:04.620 --> 00:33:07.260
like that that you may
need installed on the app

00:33:07.260 --> 00:33:08.010
scale machines.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:10.050
And then finally,
there is the task file,

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:13.920
which manages the
multi-machine process of how

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:15.780
do you get this model
on multiple machines.

00:33:15.780 --> 00:33:17.950
Let's go through
each one real quick.

00:33:17.950 --> 00:33:20.012
First up, this is
the model file.

00:33:20.012 --> 00:33:21.720
I'm not going to step
through the details

00:33:21.720 --> 00:33:22.920
of each piece of code here.

00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:25.320
We will be sharing a
blog post at the end

00:33:25.320 --> 00:33:29.400
of this video that has all
of this really well detailed.

00:33:29.400 --> 00:33:33.579
But I do want to take a moment
to highlight some key pieces

00:33:33.579 --> 00:33:34.620
here that come into play.

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:39.900
The recurring theme that
I kept harping on earlier

00:33:39.900 --> 00:33:43.020
was use the building blocks
that exist for TensorFlow,

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:44.350
don't rebuild them.

00:33:44.350 --> 00:33:47.970
This is a very good example of
that in creating the TensorFlow

00:33:47.970 --> 00:33:54.240
graph, to build an
LSTM, you should not

00:33:54.240 --> 00:33:57.280
be starting from connecting
bits of neurons together.

00:33:57.280 --> 00:33:58.380
That doesn't make sense.

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:02.340
Instead what I'm doing here
is using the basic LSTM cell

00:34:02.340 --> 00:34:04.800
that TensorFlow
already provides.

00:34:04.800 --> 00:34:06.780
To simplify my task,
I can just connect

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:09.270
together bigger
and bigger pieces

00:34:09.270 --> 00:34:11.190
to solve the larger problem.

00:34:11.190 --> 00:34:13.179
That's part of the model.

00:34:13.179 --> 00:34:15.989
Next up, at a higher
level, the task file,

00:34:15.989 --> 00:34:19.590
which does the orchestration.

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:24.210
Instead of, again, managing the
execution and split of the data

00:34:24.210 --> 00:34:27.449
across hundreds of hundreds of
machines, what we're doing here

00:34:27.449 --> 00:34:30.870
is using the
tf.learn.estimator API.

00:34:30.870 --> 00:34:35.010
This API takes care of all
of those sort of difficulties

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:38.219
for you, so that you take the
model, you point it at data,

00:34:38.219 --> 00:34:39.389
and you say, go.

00:34:39.389 --> 00:34:42.570
And the estimator API takes care
of slicing up the data sets,

00:34:42.570 --> 00:34:45.179
running them in parallel, and
combining the results for you.

00:34:45.179 --> 00:34:49.520
Again, another example of not
reinventing the wheel yourself.

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:52.409
Now that the code is
ready, you need to run it.

00:34:52.409 --> 00:34:55.409
That brings me to this
other piece of code here

00:34:55.409 --> 00:34:58.800
that actually starts the
cloud machine learning job.

00:34:58.800 --> 00:35:01.470
And what's happening here
is we're taking those files,

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:04.770
combining them into a
task called trainer.task

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:06.510
in this case right here.

00:35:06.510 --> 00:35:10.060
And we're telling Cloud
Machine Learning Engine, hey,

00:35:10.060 --> 00:35:13.140
run this in the region I
defined here, us-central1,

00:35:13.140 --> 00:35:16.470
with these other parameters
and at this scale.

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:21.060
This knob about setting
the scale for your job

00:35:21.060 --> 00:35:24.900
is something you control to
say how many resources you

00:35:24.900 --> 00:35:28.050
want to run this training on.

00:35:28.050 --> 00:35:30.240
For example, I've run
trainings where I've literally

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:34.960
requested thousands of cores
with a similar config file,

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:36.562
and it will give
that to me, and I

00:35:36.562 --> 00:35:38.520
won't have to do any
management and I will only

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:40.710
pay by the minute.

00:35:40.710 --> 00:35:42.660
So once you submit
the job, the job

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:45.480
becomes a Machine
Learning Engine

00:35:45.480 --> 00:35:48.150
job, which you can, on
the Google Cloud Console,

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:51.000
track as it's running, look
at the logs, all of that.

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:53.130
And then finally it tells
you how many ML units

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:55.090
it consumed at
the end of the job

00:35:55.090 --> 00:35:57.540
so that you always know
what each specific training

00:35:57.540 --> 00:36:00.090
event costs you.

00:36:00.090 --> 00:36:02.370
The output of this job
is a trained model,

00:36:02.370 --> 00:36:07.380
which we set the target
output directory as a flag.

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:09.520
That's what this
directory looks like.

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:11.550
And there's a bunch
of extraneous files

00:36:11.550 --> 00:36:15.540
here generally concerned
around resuming

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:16.740
training and all of that.

00:36:16.740 --> 00:36:21.320
The file in question
here is the graph.pbtxt.

00:36:21.320 --> 00:36:25.950
This file is the entirety,
effectively, of your TensorFlow

00:36:25.950 --> 00:36:28.650
model, including the
structure, and the weights,

00:36:28.650 --> 00:36:30.160
and all of that.

00:36:30.160 --> 00:36:33.120
So now that you have that, Cloud
Machine Learning Engine also

00:36:33.120 --> 00:36:35.460
has a way to, just like it
had a training job, has a way

00:36:35.460 --> 00:36:38.705
to host files for you in
an auto scaling platform,

00:36:38.705 --> 00:36:40.080
so you never have
to worry about,

00:36:40.080 --> 00:36:42.960
how do I actually
use this model?

00:36:42.960 --> 00:36:46.140
So let's move over
to a Cloud Function

00:36:46.140 --> 00:36:49.681
that I have written
that let's you,

00:36:49.681 --> 00:36:51.180
every time there's
a new temperature

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:55.470
reading from the device,
this function gets triggered.

00:36:55.470 --> 00:36:59.010
What it will do then is
grab the last nine readings

00:36:59.010 --> 00:37:01.440
from BigQuery, going
back to the work that

00:37:01.440 --> 00:37:04.020
was done in Webinar 2.

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:05.880
That data is then
used to predict

00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:10.350
what should be the 10th
reading according to the model.

00:37:10.350 --> 00:37:15.000
So we ask Google ML Engine to
create that prediction based

00:37:15.000 --> 00:37:17.130
on the model we had given it.

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:20.610
That prediction is then compared
to the actual 10th prediction

00:37:20.610 --> 00:37:22.230
that triggered this function.

00:37:22.230 --> 00:37:25.420
We look at the delta, in this
case, a very simple threshold.

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:27.420
And if the threshold
is exceeded,

00:37:27.420 --> 00:37:30.930
we log that there
was an anomaly.

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:34.830
Again, in this case you could
do whatever other action

00:37:34.830 --> 00:37:36.940
that was needed in the
case of an anomaly.

00:37:36.940 --> 00:37:39.220
So this was running
in the cloud.

00:37:39.220 --> 00:37:43.110
But with IoT, you might
not have good low-latency,

00:37:43.110 --> 00:37:45.280
reliable connectivity.

00:37:45.280 --> 00:37:46.464
How do you solve for that?

00:37:46.464 --> 00:37:48.630
And the way you do that is
you move the model closer

00:37:48.630 --> 00:37:52.330
to the edge onto your gateways,
onto your edge devices.

00:37:52.330 --> 00:37:54.884
So Martin, you want to talk
about that a little bit?

00:37:54.884 --> 00:37:55.800
MARTIN KRONBERG: Sure.

00:37:55.800 --> 00:37:57.540
What we can look
at here is a bit

00:37:57.540 --> 00:38:01.200
of a hybrid model,
where we train our ML

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:05.250
model in the cloud using
the Google Cloud APIs

00:38:05.250 --> 00:38:08.880
and then run that model
locally on the gateway.

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:11.160
So like you mentioned earlier,
training on an ML model

00:38:11.160 --> 00:38:13.680
requires a huge amount
of compute power

00:38:13.680 --> 00:38:17.340
and way more than is going to
be available even on the highest

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:19.500
end gateway-type device.

00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:22.050
So we can offload that
task to the cloud,

00:38:22.050 --> 00:38:25.489
where we have access
to all that power,

00:38:25.489 --> 00:38:27.780
we can set up the sensor
data, we can run the training,

00:38:27.780 --> 00:38:30.210
and we can generate
a training model.

00:38:30.210 --> 00:38:32.730
So instead of running
that model in the cloud,

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:34.730
we can run that model locally.

00:38:34.730 --> 00:38:36.270
And running the
model locally means

00:38:36.270 --> 00:38:40.000
that you have to set up your
compute environment locally.

00:38:40.000 --> 00:38:42.840
And you're also going to have to
be limited by the compute power

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:45.990
that you have available
on your local device.

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:48.930
However, the trade-off being
that you are no longer bound

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:52.110
to the cloud service
to run your model.

00:38:52.110 --> 00:38:54.660
So let's say that our
application uses an ML

00:38:54.660 --> 00:38:58.080
model to run a real-time
control system of some kind that

00:38:58.080 --> 00:39:01.680
has a low tolerance for
any kind of latency.

00:39:01.680 --> 00:39:03.570
In that case, by running
the model locally

00:39:03.570 --> 00:39:06.690
we can avoid having to deal
with any latency issues that

00:39:06.690 --> 00:39:10.350
can arise from leveraging a
planet-scale cloud analytics

00:39:10.350 --> 00:39:12.330
platform like GCP.

00:39:12.330 --> 00:39:15.065
Likewise, if we're using our
model to run a control system,

00:39:15.065 --> 00:39:16.440
we don't want that
control system

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:19.260
to stop functioning completely
if it loses connection

00:39:19.260 --> 00:39:20.632
to the internet.

00:39:20.632 --> 00:39:23.310
I can imagine something
like that's being used for,

00:39:23.310 --> 00:39:25.770
let's say, monitoring
a trade that

00:39:25.770 --> 00:39:28.350
can pass through regions
without internet connectivity

00:39:28.350 --> 00:39:32.250
or just go through a tunnel
and lose service completely.

00:39:32.250 --> 00:39:34.230
Once again, if we run
the model locally,

00:39:34.230 --> 00:39:36.880
we won't have to worry about
losing our control system

00:39:36.880 --> 00:39:38.730
if we lose connectivity.

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:42.420
Finally, there could be an
issue of the amount of raw data

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:45.030
that is being fed
into our model.

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:47.670
In this example, we're passing
it a single temperature reading

00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:51.456
once a second, which is a
fairly trivial amount of data.

00:39:51.456 --> 00:39:53.520
But we can imagine
an application

00:39:53.520 --> 00:39:57.030
where we actually have to send
a non-trivial amount of data

00:39:57.030 --> 00:39:59.340
to the model for processing.

00:39:59.340 --> 00:40:02.190
If instead of a single
reading once a second,

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:06.690
you have to send 100 readings
or 1,000 readings once a second,

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:09.000
you can start to run
into bandwidth issues.

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:11.640
So once again, running
the model locally,

00:40:11.640 --> 00:40:14.390
we can get around those issues.

00:40:14.390 --> 00:40:16.140
So that's kind of the
reasoning behind why

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:19.290
we might want to run a trained
model locally, and let's

00:40:19.290 --> 00:40:22.770
just take a quick look at how
we can do that on our gateway.

00:40:22.770 --> 00:40:26.940
We can run our trained model on
a local instance of TensorFlow.

00:40:26.940 --> 00:40:29.610
And since our gateway
is running Ubuntu,

00:40:29.610 --> 00:40:32.047
and TensorFlow is an
open-source platform,

00:40:32.047 --> 00:40:33.630
there's really no
issues installing it

00:40:33.630 --> 00:40:35.520
and getting it running.

00:40:35.520 --> 00:40:38.380
You can get your environment
set up in a few ways.

00:40:38.380 --> 00:40:41.490
So first you can use pip to
just install the Python modules

00:40:41.490 --> 00:40:43.020
in the user environment.

00:40:43.020 --> 00:40:45.090
We can also use a
virtual environment

00:40:45.090 --> 00:40:47.460
to create an isolated
Python environment

00:40:47.460 --> 00:40:49.832
and install TensorFlow
inside that.

00:40:49.832 --> 00:40:51.540
And finally, we can
use Docker to install

00:40:51.540 --> 00:40:54.780
an entire isolated Docker
image with TensorFlow

00:40:54.780 --> 00:40:56.772
installed inside.

00:40:56.772 --> 00:40:58.230
I would say that
if you are running

00:40:58.230 --> 00:41:00.330
many Python applications
on your gateway,

00:41:00.330 --> 00:41:04.230
then an isolated environment
is the preferred way of going.

00:41:04.230 --> 00:41:06.180
But if you're only
running TensorFlow,

00:41:06.180 --> 00:41:09.510
the simpler local install is
probably what you'd want to do.

00:41:09.510 --> 00:41:11.670
Now we're ready
to run our model.

00:41:11.670 --> 00:41:14.220
When training our model, we
generate a protocol buffer

00:41:14.220 --> 00:41:18.150
file, or a .pb, which
contains the graph data about

00:41:18.150 --> 00:41:21.020
our models, as well as the
weights, the gradients,

00:41:21.020 --> 00:41:23.670
the biases, basically all
the information that we need

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:26.190
to recreate that neural network.

00:41:26.190 --> 00:41:28.260
By loading file
into TensorFlow, we

00:41:28.260 --> 00:41:31.110
can rebuild that model that
was trained in the cloud

00:41:31.110 --> 00:41:34.729
and then start passing
sensor data into that model.

00:41:34.729 --> 00:41:36.270
It's going to monitor
the sensor data

00:41:36.270 --> 00:41:38.640
and predict what it thinks
the next temperature

00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:40.620
value is going to be.

00:41:40.620 --> 00:41:42.120
And we can load all
these parameters

00:41:42.120 --> 00:41:46.380
into TensorFlow using
a load module API.

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:49.320
Once the model is loaded, we can
run our temperature data array

00:41:49.320 --> 00:41:50.790
through the model.

00:41:50.790 --> 00:41:52.320
We read the
temperature data using

00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:58.120
our sensor API, temp.read,
and then we run our model.

00:41:58.120 --> 00:42:00.750
The model is going to output
a predicted next temperature

00:42:00.750 --> 00:42:04.440
value based on nine previous
temperature readings.

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:07.920
We can compare the predicted
value to the actual reading

00:42:07.920 --> 00:42:10.170
and decide if that
delta is high enough

00:42:10.170 --> 00:42:13.314
to label it as an anomaly.

00:42:13.314 --> 00:42:14.730
Now, keep in mind
that we're going

00:42:14.730 --> 00:42:17.820
to have to match the data format
that the model was trained

00:42:17.820 --> 00:42:18.630
with.

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:20.940
So we're going to pass in
an array of nine temperature

00:42:20.940 --> 00:42:22.800
values every single
time in order

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:24.650
to get an output
for the 10th value.

00:42:24.650 --> 00:42:25.650
KIRAT PANDYA: All right.

00:42:25.650 --> 00:42:27.780
So that's all we have
for today, folks.

00:42:27.780 --> 00:42:29.490
We hope that this
session really helps

00:42:29.490 --> 00:42:32.460
you start solving real
business problems with machine

00:42:32.460 --> 00:42:34.020
learning--

00:42:34.020 --> 00:42:35.880
start your journey,
get machine learning

00:42:35.880 --> 00:42:37.466
implemented into your business.

00:42:37.466 --> 00:42:39.840
MARTIN KRONBERG: Yeah, and
thank you again for tuning in.

00:42:39.840 --> 00:42:42.450
And I really hope that this
whole series of webinars

00:42:42.450 --> 00:42:44.180
really inspires you
to go out and create

00:42:44.180 --> 00:42:46.500
IoT solutions for yourselves.

00:42:46.500 --> 00:42:47.766
We'll see you guys next time.

00:42:47.766 --> 00:42:50.682
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:42:58.944 --> 00:43:00.150
KIRAT PANDYA: All right.

00:43:00.150 --> 00:43:01.160
Welcome back.

00:43:01.160 --> 00:43:05.220
Hopefully the material in that
recording was helpful to you.

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:06.650
So we've collected
some questions

00:43:06.650 --> 00:43:09.750
along the way that came up in
the live chat and otherwise.

00:43:09.750 --> 00:43:12.530
So let's just jump
right into them.

00:43:12.530 --> 00:43:14.660
The first one was
how much data do

00:43:14.660 --> 00:43:16.490
you need to train
a model like this,

00:43:16.490 --> 00:43:18.560
particularly around LSTMs?

00:43:18.560 --> 00:43:22.880
So the way to think about
the data requirements are

00:43:22.880 --> 00:43:24.890
think about the periodicity
that you're trying

00:43:24.890 --> 00:43:26.310
to build into your model.

00:43:26.310 --> 00:43:29.360
So for example, if you
have a temperature sensor

00:43:29.360 --> 00:43:31.450
in a room that is very
climate-controlled,

00:43:31.450 --> 00:43:34.400
that has very narrow ranges
that don't change a lot,

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:37.520
then you need a short window
of data to train this model.

00:43:37.520 --> 00:43:40.850
And then it will detect
anytime there is an anomaly.

00:43:40.850 --> 00:43:43.160
If you've got, say, a
temperature sensor inside

00:43:43.160 --> 00:43:46.466
a refrigerated truck that
sees significant shifts

00:43:46.466 --> 00:43:48.590
in temperature-- the air
conditioning is turned off

00:43:48.590 --> 00:43:50.590
at night when it's parked,
all of those things--

00:43:50.590 --> 00:43:53.000
then you will need
more data over time

00:43:53.000 --> 00:43:57.110
as this truck travels the United
States, or whatever it may be,

00:43:57.110 --> 00:44:01.310
to build that periodicity
back into the data.

00:44:01.310 --> 00:44:03.799
So there isn't a strict sort
of megabytes, gigabytes,

00:44:03.799 --> 00:44:04.715
terabytes requirement.

00:44:04.715 --> 00:44:08.834
It's more about what
you're trying to model.

00:44:08.834 --> 00:44:10.500
SUNDAR SUBRAMANIAM:
Here's another one--

00:44:10.500 --> 00:44:14.520
what kind of sensors do the
Intel sensor libraries support?

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:18.960
The UPM sensor library supports
about 400 different sensors

00:44:18.960 --> 00:44:21.870
and actuators, from
low end all the way

00:44:21.870 --> 00:44:25.200
to industrial, commercial
grade [INAUDIBLE] sensors.

00:44:25.200 --> 00:44:28.380
You can monitor a variety
of environment variables--

00:44:28.380 --> 00:44:32.594
temperature, pressure, gas,
or moisture, and so on--

00:44:32.594 --> 00:44:34.260
And over different
kinds different kinds

00:44:34.260 --> 00:44:36.180
of protocols as well--

00:44:36.180 --> 00:44:42.690
Wi-Fi, Z-Wave, Zigbee,
BLE, USB, Modbus et cetera.

00:44:42.690 --> 00:44:47.010
So take a look at
upm.mraa.io, and you'll

00:44:47.010 --> 00:44:48.870
get a lot more
information about what

00:44:48.870 --> 00:44:50.300
these libraries can do for you.

00:44:50.300 --> 00:44:51.480
KIRAT PANDYA: Awesome.

00:44:51.480 --> 00:44:54.120
All right, next up-- so
what are some good resources

00:44:54.120 --> 00:44:57.820
to learn modern machine
learning and deep learning?

00:44:57.820 --> 00:45:00.660
So I'd say a couple
of things to do.

00:45:00.660 --> 00:45:06.250
Number one-- Coursera has
few courses on deep learning.

00:45:06.250 --> 00:45:07.950
But what I suggest
you do is you start

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:11.850
from the older sort of
machine learning course

00:45:11.850 --> 00:45:13.710
that's available on Coursera.

00:45:13.710 --> 00:45:16.440
What it'll do is it'll help
you build a good foundation

00:45:16.440 --> 00:45:19.050
around what are the sort
of traditional techniques

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:22.410
that were around before neural
networks came along and became

00:45:22.410 --> 00:45:23.190
more popular--

00:45:23.190 --> 00:45:25.230
not even came along,
became more popular--

00:45:25.230 --> 00:45:27.550
and then how they're related.

00:45:27.550 --> 00:45:29.670
And then from there you
can do the deep learning

00:45:29.670 --> 00:45:32.640
course that was recently
launched on Coursera.

00:45:32.640 --> 00:45:35.370
And I think that's a very good
sort of ramp-up to then figure

00:45:35.370 --> 00:45:38.010
out where you want to go
deep, because the field is

00:45:38.010 --> 00:45:39.990
really wide and really deep.

00:45:39.990 --> 00:45:42.750
The other thing I
would add is Google

00:45:42.750 --> 00:45:45.280
has what we call
codelabs available.

00:45:45.280 --> 00:45:47.700
So if you just search
TensorFlow codelab,

00:45:47.700 --> 00:45:49.350
one of the first
results should be

00:45:49.350 --> 00:45:53.220
a code lab that is for
TensorFlow without a PhD,

00:45:53.220 --> 00:45:54.136
if you will.

00:45:54.136 --> 00:45:56.010
And that's a great start
because it gives you

00:45:56.010 --> 00:45:58.200
a very quick rundown
on everything

00:45:58.200 --> 00:45:59.670
you learn from the
Coursera course,

00:45:59.670 --> 00:46:03.030
how it applies with
real TensorFlow code.

00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:05.440
So that's where I would
say you should start.

00:46:05.440 --> 00:46:06.481
SUNDAR SUBRAMANIAM: Cool.

00:46:10.440 --> 00:46:12.600
So the next
question, I think, is

00:46:12.600 --> 00:46:14.730
more relevant for
what you're covering,

00:46:14.730 --> 00:46:19.020
which is is it is one model
per sensor, or is there a way

00:46:19.020 --> 00:46:23.610
to build a model for temperature
sensors across a facility?

00:46:23.610 --> 00:46:25.390
So sure, I can take that one.

00:46:25.390 --> 00:46:28.320
So the way I think that you
should think about that again

00:46:28.320 --> 00:46:32.100
is it's not one
model per sensor,

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:34.890
but what you're, again,
going back in especially

00:46:34.890 --> 00:46:38.010
in an LSTM kind of pattern,
what you're trying to build

00:46:38.010 --> 00:46:41.280
is a coherent model
of what's happening

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:43.350
in the real world over time.

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:45.720
So you could do one
model per sensor

00:46:45.720 --> 00:46:47.520
if you're deploying
this on each sensor

00:46:47.520 --> 00:46:50.430
and then do some
training at the sensor.

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:53.100
Or the other pattern
is collect groups

00:46:53.100 --> 00:46:56.880
of sensors that observe
a connected environment

00:46:56.880 --> 00:46:57.510
in some source.

00:46:57.510 --> 00:46:59.080
That pattern works really well.

00:46:59.080 --> 00:47:01.620
So again, if you have
sensors across a warehouse,

00:47:01.620 --> 00:47:04.290
you can take all of this
data and effectively,

00:47:04.290 --> 00:47:08.070
where I was putting
in the code you saw,

00:47:08.070 --> 00:47:10.230
basically a vector of
time of temperature

00:47:10.230 --> 00:47:12.690
measurements for one
sensor, it would be a matrix

00:47:12.690 --> 00:47:14.310
of measurements across time.

00:47:14.310 --> 00:47:16.210
And then you could
train a model that way.

00:47:16.210 --> 00:47:18.300
But again, sort of start
from the perspective

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:21.529
of what environment
am I trying to model?

00:47:21.529 --> 00:47:23.070
And then go from
there, and then pick

00:47:23.070 --> 00:47:24.194
the right tool for the job.

00:47:26.790 --> 00:47:28.370
All right.

00:47:28.370 --> 00:47:31.250
SUNDAR SUBRAMANIAM: So I
want to use a microphone

00:47:31.250 --> 00:47:36.350
to do streaming voice to
text locally using a NUC.

00:47:36.350 --> 00:47:38.240
What's the best way to do that?

00:47:38.240 --> 00:47:40.120
That sounds like an
interesting project.

00:47:40.120 --> 00:47:41.390
KIRAT PANDYA: Yeah.

00:47:41.390 --> 00:47:42.890
SUNDAR SUBRAMANIAM:
So one way to do

00:47:42.890 --> 00:47:48.345
it would be to pick a microphone
that has support for Ubuntu.

00:47:48.345 --> 00:47:51.680
Pick a microphone that
has a USB support,

00:47:51.680 --> 00:47:53.900
and then plug it into the NUC.

00:47:53.900 --> 00:47:58.790
You also want a recording
software to record the audio

00:47:58.790 --> 00:48:02.780
and then send the audio
chunks to the Google ML API.

00:48:02.780 --> 00:48:06.380
In the webinar-- and if
you're sticking with Python,

00:48:06.380 --> 00:48:08.700
you can use the PyAudio.

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:09.920
That would work.

00:48:09.920 --> 00:48:12.470
And then you can chunk
the audio and then

00:48:12.470 --> 00:48:15.980
send them to the ML APIs.

00:48:15.980 --> 00:48:18.320
But one thing that I'd
recommend is make sure

00:48:18.320 --> 00:48:21.170
that the samples
are long enough.

00:48:21.170 --> 00:48:24.080
So I'd probably say about
100 milliseconds or longer

00:48:24.080 --> 00:48:25.520
would be a good idea.

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:26.830
But good luck with the project.

00:48:26.830 --> 00:48:28.788
KIRAT PANDYA: Yeah, and
I'd add one more thing.

00:48:28.788 --> 00:48:31.550
The API supports both
synchronous and asynchronous

00:48:31.550 --> 00:48:34.070
audio, so if you want to
do something like a Google

00:48:34.070 --> 00:48:36.374
Assistant scenario
where it's live,

00:48:36.374 --> 00:48:37.790
happening right
in the moment, you

00:48:37.790 --> 00:48:39.860
can use the
synchronous API, or you

00:48:39.860 --> 00:48:42.800
can use the asynchronous
API for larger chunks

00:48:42.800 --> 00:48:45.170
of prerecorded audio that
you're trying to transcribe.

00:48:45.170 --> 00:48:45.590
SUNDAR SUBRAMANIAM: Perfect.

00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:47.090
KIRAT PANDYA: So
it works both ways.

00:48:47.090 --> 00:48:47.600
All right.

00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:50.690
Next up is, what are the
resource requirements

00:48:50.690 --> 00:48:53.300
to run TensorFlow at the
edge, in a scenario like this

00:48:53.300 --> 00:48:55.910
on an edge gateway?

00:48:55.910 --> 00:48:58.970
The way I'd answer that is
I think the purer number

00:48:58.970 --> 00:49:01.280
people think about it is,
oh, I can measure FLOPs.

00:49:01.280 --> 00:49:05.140
Doesn't really work that
way, because model complexity

00:49:05.140 --> 00:49:06.800
and the model type
itself drives a lot

00:49:06.800 --> 00:49:08.120
of your resource requirements.

00:49:08.120 --> 00:49:09.980
So what I would
say is figure out

00:49:09.980 --> 00:49:13.290
the problem you're
trying to solve add up

00:49:13.290 --> 00:49:15.800
sort of small, medium,
large budget of hey,

00:49:15.800 --> 00:49:19.100
I'm running a big server
at the edge versus I'm

00:49:19.100 --> 00:49:20.330
running a low-power device.

00:49:20.330 --> 00:49:21.080
Start from that.

00:49:21.080 --> 00:49:22.730
That sort of gives
you a constraint

00:49:22.730 --> 00:49:25.442
on am I doing hundreds
of thousands, millions,

00:49:25.442 --> 00:49:26.900
or hundreds of
millions of neurons.

00:49:26.900 --> 00:49:28.358
You're starting to
think like that.

00:49:28.358 --> 00:49:31.100
And then from there,
benchmark and iterate.

00:49:31.100 --> 00:49:36.560
And what you'll find is that,
if you had done a FLOP space

00:49:36.560 --> 00:49:38.720
measurement, it
doesn't scale linearly,

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:41.810
because as your compute
at the edge goes down,

00:49:41.810 --> 00:49:44.360
different bottlenecks show
up, like memory, bandwidth,

00:49:44.360 --> 00:49:49.620
and things like that that would
impact how well the model runs.

00:49:49.620 --> 00:49:51.260
And then the other
side of the equation

00:49:51.260 --> 00:49:54.760
is also think about if you
don't need your inference

00:49:54.760 --> 00:49:56.950
to happen in 30
milliseconds, then

00:49:56.950 --> 00:49:59.920
that really frees you up in
terms of how much compute

00:49:59.920 --> 00:50:01.840
you need at the edge.

00:50:01.840 --> 00:50:04.510
We have scenarios where
we've deployed effectively

00:50:04.510 --> 00:50:05.890
a Raspberry Pi.

00:50:05.890 --> 00:50:08.980
And it runs super slow,
but it was good enough

00:50:08.980 --> 00:50:11.260
for that scenario-- six
seconds to do inference.

00:50:11.260 --> 00:50:13.610
But that was good enough
for what we were doing.

00:50:13.610 --> 00:50:16.900
So think of it that way,
And then test and iterate.

00:50:16.900 --> 00:50:18.640
And then shrink your
model as and when

00:50:18.640 --> 00:50:22.150
necessary, trading off accuracy,
basically your F1 score,

00:50:22.150 --> 00:50:26.076
against the compute
requirements.

00:50:26.076 --> 00:50:27.190
All right.

00:50:27.190 --> 00:50:28.120
Looks like--

